// This file gives the dates when it is believed each of the chapters of the
// Old Testament and New Testament were written.
//
// Once you have stripped off these head-of-file comments, this file is in JSON
// format.
//
// The file structure is as follows:
//
// - There is a separate record for each book.
//
// - Within each book, there is a separate record for each chapter.
//
// - For each chapter there are two dates:
//
//   . With the exception of Job, all chapters have an associated Ussher date.
//     These are taken from the KJV Bible system by Dr William Lloyd (Bishop of
//     Worcester) in 1701.  They sometimes differ a little from Ussher's
//     publication.
//
//   . In addition, with the exception of a few very early dates, all chapters
//     have the date ascribed to them by contemporary scholarly opinion.
//     Very early dates use the Anno Mundi (AM) scheme used in Jewish
//     scholarship.  This assigns Year 0 to the Creation.  Where dates are
//     the subject of particular debate, they are specifically marked
//     below as approximate.  (These 'approx' markers refer _only_ to the
//     scholarly dates -- Ussher did not mark any dates in this way.)
//
//
// AM and AD dates are shown as positive, BC dates as negative.  This means
// that if you wish to follow the Ussher scheme, ordering based upon the
// numeric values of the dates will give the chapters in order.  The same is
// _almost_ true of the scholarly dates, with the exception of dates marked
// as AM.
//
// Chapters follow the KJV versification scheme.
//
// Bear in mind that there _are_ differences of opinion as to the dates,
// and there are alternative dating schemes covering some portions of the
// Bible text.  You might care to take a look at
//
